# React Frontend Rules

## Component Standards
- Functional components only
- Props interface defined above component
- Memoize with React.memo() when appropriate
- Use named exports

## TypeScript Standards
- No `any` types - use `unknown` if type truly unknown
- Define interfaces for all props, state, API responses
- Use enums for fixed value sets
- Prefer `type` for unions, `interface` for objects

## Hook Standards
- Custom hooks start with `use` prefix
- Extract complex state logic to custom hooks
- Use useCallback for event handlers passed to children
- Use useMemo for expensive computations

## API Integration
- Use axios or fetch with typed responses
- Handle loading, error, success states
- Use React Query or SWR for caching (if added)
- Centralize API calls in services/ folder

## Component Pattern
```tsx
interface ContractorCardProps {
  contractor: Contractor;
  onSelect: (id: number) => void;
}

export const ContractorCard = memo(function ContractorCard({
  contractor,
  onSelect,
}: ContractorCardProps) {
  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
    onSelect(contractor.id);
  }, [contractor.id, onSelect]);

  return (
    <Card onClick={handleClick}>
      <Typography>{contractor.company_name}</Typography>
    </Card>
  );
});
```

## State Management
- Use React Context for global state (auth, theme)
- Use useState for component-local state
- Use useReducer for complex state logic
- Avoid prop drilling > 2 levels deep

## Error Handling
- Use Error Boundaries for component errors
- Display user-friendly error messages
- Log errors for debugging
- Provide retry mechanisms where appropriate

## Line of Code 
- component code not exceed the 250 line 
- use modularize approach